# Wildcards — `? extends T`

## Generics are invariant

**`List<Integer>` is NOT a `List<Number>`** — even though `Integer` is a `Number`.

```java
List<Number> nums = ints;   // if this compiled…
nums.add(3.14);             // …a Double into a List<Integer>!
```

Invariance forbids it to prevent exactly that.

## `? extends` — an upper bound

```java
double sum(List<? extends Number> list) {   // accepts List<Integer>, List<Double>…
    double t = 0;
    for (Number n : list) t += n.doubleValue();
    return t;
}
```

## Read yes, write no

```java
Number n = list.get(0);   // ✅ certainly a Number
list.add(1);              // ❌ Integer? Double? can't be sure
```

## Mental model: a **producer**

`? extends T` = data flows **out** — you read/iterate/copy-from it, never add (except `null`).
Used by `Collections.max`, `stream.map` inputs.
